'''''Ternstroemia''''' is a large genus of [[flowering plant]]s in the family [[Pentaphylacaceae]], distributed throughout tropical and subtropical regions in Africa, Asia, and the Americas. The genus, first described by [[Carl Linnaeus the Younger]] in 1782, consists of 161 species of shrubs and trees generally known as '''ternstroemias'''.{{R|POWO}}{{R|USDA}} Members of the genus are often grown as [[ornamental plant]]s or are used medicinally.{{R|MBG}}<ref name=":0">{{cite web |url=https://tropical.theferns.info/viewtropical.php?id=Ternstroemia+tepezapote |title=''Ternstroemia tepezapote'' Schltdl. & Cham. |website=Useful Tropical Plants |access-date=2 June 2026}}</ref>

==Description==
Members of ''Ternstroemia'' grow as evergreen shrubs or trees with {{plantgloss|alternate}} leaves, whose margins are [[Entire leaf|entire]] or uncommonly [[Serrate leaf|serrate]]. The flowers are {{plantgloss|axillary}} and usually solitary, but may appear in clusters on leafless branches. Species of the genus are {{plantgloss|androdioecious}}, where bisexual and male flowers appear on separate individuals. The fruits are fleshy, berry-like [[Capsule (fruit)|capsule]]s that either remain closed or [[dehisce]], revealing the dangling seeds within; the seeds are kidney‑shaped, slightly compressed, and covered by a fleshy red coating over a thick [[endosperm]].{{R|TSO}}{{R|FoC}}{{R|WFO}}

==Taxonomy==
''Ternstroemia'' was first described by Carl Linnaeus the Younger in 1782, having been proposed by [[José Celestino Mutis]] earlier.{{R|POWO}} The genus, along with related genera such as ''[[Eurya]]'' and ''[[Cleyera]]'', were previously classified within a subfamily of [[Theaceae]]; in a subsequent revision, the genus, together with its relatives, were transferred to the now defunct Ternstroemiaceae family. In 2016, the [[APG IV system]] placed the group in the formerly [[monotypic]] Pentaphylacaceae.{{R|TSO}}

The genus name is in honor of [[Christopher Tärnström]], a clergyman, botanist, and student of [[Linnaeus]].<ref>{{Cite book |last1=Quattrocchi |first1=Umberto |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=zIOvJSJs-IkC&lpg=PA2254&pg=PA2646 |title=CRC World Dictionary of Plant Names: Common Names, Scientific Names, Eponyms. Synonyms, and Etymology |publisher=[[CRC Press]] |year=1999 |isbn=978-0-8493-2678-3 |volume=4 |page=2646 |access-date=2 June 2026}}</ref>

